   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to the definition of \"in need of involuntary care and treatment\" and to whom an application for such admission is made",
    "basePrintNo": "S6154",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6154-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§15.01 & 15.27, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6154\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation\nto the definition of \"in need of involuntary care and treatment\" and\nto whom an application for such admission is made\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis bill would amend the Mental Hygiene Law (MHL) to ensure the\nstatutory definition of \"in need of involuntary care and treatment\"\nconforms to constitutional due process standards with respect to\ninvoluntary medical admissions.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of this bill would amend MHL § 15.01 to clarify that the\ndefinition of \"in need of involuntary care and treatment\" expressly\nincludes the requirement that the person being involuntarily confined\nposes a real and present threat of substantial harm to themselves or\nothers.\n\nSection 2 of the bill would amend MHL § 15.27 by deleting the\nreference in subsection (a) to \"the director of a school\" and replace\nit with \"the commissioner.\"\n\nSection 3 would provide for an immediate effective date.\n\n STATEMENT IN SUPPORT :\n\nThe language of MHL § 15.01 does not explicitly require that the\nperson must pose a substantial physical danger to themselves or others\nin order to be involuntarily confined. In order to comply with\nconstitutional due process requirements, however, New York State\ncourts as well as the Office of People with Developmental Disabilities\n(OPWDD) have interpreted this statute to require a showing of\nsubstantial physical danger to self or others prior to ordering\ninvoluntary confinement.\n\nThis bill would therefore amend MHL § 15.01 to expressly require that\nan involuntary confinement determination for purposes of care and\ntreatment \"must be based upon a finding that the person to be\ncommitted poses a real and present threat of substantial harm to\nthemselves or others\" (see In Re Scopes, 59 AD2d 203 (3d Dept. 1977)\nand Matter of Harry M., 96 AD2d 201 (2d Dept. 1983)). This would\nenhance public safety while protecting the constitutional rights of\nindividuals who require involuntary care and treatment.\n\nSecond, when a person requires involuntary care and treatment, MHL §\n15.27 currently allows the director of a school (e.g., developmental\ncenter) to receive and retain such person in the school. The detention\nmust be supported by a formal application and the certification of two\nphysicians or a physician and a certified psychologist. However, no\nguidance is provided to clarify which of OPWDD's multiple directors\nshould receive the application. To address this concern, this bill\nwould provide that the OPWDD Commissioner, rather than an unspecified\ndirector, may decide an application for individual involuntary care\nand treatment. This would eliminate any uncertainty as to where an\napplication should be submitted and reflects the centralization by\nOPWDD of such decisions.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new proposal.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis bill would take effect immediately upon signing.\n",
